---
import CodeBlock from '../../../components/CodeBlock.astro'
import Explainer from '../../../components/Explainer.astro'
import { snippets } from '../../../data/components.js'
import { diagrams } from '../../../data/diagrams.js'
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro'
import { sitePath } from '../../../lib/paths.js'

const repo = 'https://github.com/skyaara/suitecut/blob/main'

const layout = `suitecut-browser.app/      the engine, with the CEF framework and five helper apps
suitecut-session           the session service, protocols 0 and 1
licenses/                  CEF, Chromium credits, libyuv, suitecut, OpenSSL, curl, libpsl, brotli
kit.json                   versions, protocols, signing, and every file's path, size and sha256`

const verify = `python3 scripts/verify-embed-kit-macos.py --kit .suitecut/embed-kit \\
  --client-build .suitecut/kit-client-build --output .suitecut/kit-verify-NEW`

type Row = readonly [string, string]

const control: Row[] = [
  ['unclaimed', 'Nobody holds the tab'],
  ['agent', 'The agent holds it; a person\u2019s input or navigation takes it'],
  [
    'human',
    'The person holds it; agent calls are refused with human_in_control until they release it',
  ],
  [
    'generation',
    'Raised on every change of hands; an action against an old one gets stale_generation',
  ],
]
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Embedding"
  description="Embed the suitecut engine in a macOS app with the embed kit and suitecut/embed.h: sessions, shared tabs, GPU frames and an agent working beside a person."
  section="embedding"
>
  <h1>Embedding</h1>
  <p>
    The macOS embed kit puts the suitecut engine inside another app. The app opens a session, shows
    the engine's tabs in its own views and can let an AI agent work in the same tabs. The kit is one
    folder of signed files that the app copies into its own bundle; there is no separate browser to
    install.
  </p>
  <Explainer explained={diagrams.embed} />

  <h2 id="kit">What's in the kit</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={layout} label="Layout" lang="plaintext" />
  <p>
    The engine stays a full <code>.app</code> bundle, because Chromium's helper processes only find the
    browser when its main executable is bundled. The kit's Mach-O files link only
    <code>/usr/lib</code> and <code>/System/Library</code> and need macOS 14 or newer. They are signed
    with the hardened runtime and a timestamp; the kit is not notarized.
  </p>
  <p>
    The kit defaults to the official CEF 156.0.30 build, which plays no H.264, AAC or HEVC.
    <code>--cef codecs</code> packages the third-party codec build instead, which has no codec patent
    licence. The files total 333 MB, most of it the CEF framework.
  </p>

  <h2 id="build">Build and check it</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href={`${repo}/docs/embedding/embed-kit-macos.md#recipe`}>full recipe</a> builds static curl,
    OpenSSL, libpsl and brotli for macOS 14, then the engine, the session service and the kit. The last
    steps package it and check it:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={snippets.kitStart} label="Terminal" lang="bash" />
  <CodeBlock code={verify} label="Terminal" lang="bash" />

  <h2 id="api">The embed API</h2>
  <p>
    <code>suitecut/embed.h</code> is a C header in the same <code>SuiteCut::Client</code> library as
    <code>client.h</code>. It speaks session protocol 1 to <code>suitecut-session</code> over a private
    socket.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={snippets.embedCode} label="C" lang="cpp" />
  <ul class="dp-rows">
    <li>
      <code>sc_runtime_inspect</code>
      <span class="dp-detail"
        >Checks the code signature, then asks the service for its version and protocol range</span
      >
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>sc_session_open</code>
      <span class="dp-detail"
        ><code>SC_SESSION_START</code> starts the service; <code>SC_SESSION_PROMPTS</code> holds the owner's
        dialogs for the app to answer</span
      >
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>sc_session_grant</code>
      <span class="dp-detail"
        >Gives an agent chosen tool groups for a set lifetime; <code>sc_session_revoke</code> takes them
        back</span
      >
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>sc_tab_open</code>
      <span class="dp-detail"
        >Opens a tab; <code>sc_tab_take</code> and <code>sc_tab_release</code> change who holds it</span
      >
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>sc_view_attach</code>
      <span class="dp-detail"
        >Attaches a view to a tab; <code>sc_view_next_frame</code> returns its newest frame</span
      >
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="frames">Frames</h2>
  <p>
    A frame is an <code>IOSurfaceRef</code> in NV12. The engine converts each paint with Metal into a
    surface it owns, so frames stay on the GPU on the way to the app. The library keeps only the newest
    waiting frame and drops frames from any process but the engine. The app draws the surface and hands
    it back with <code>sc_frame_release</code>. Off macOS, frame sinks return
    <code>SC_UNSUPPORTED</code>; a view without a sink still takes input and resizing.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sharing">Sharing a tab with an agent</h2>
  <Explainer explained={diagrams.together} />
  <ul class="dp-rows">
    {
      control.map(([key, value]) => (
        <li>
          <code>{key}</code>
          <span class="dp-detail">{value}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
  <p>
    An agent connects as the session's agent and gets the tool groups the app granted. The
    <a href={sitePath('/docs/agents')}>agent tools</a> are the same <code>observe</code>,
    <code>act</code> and <code>wait</code> that <code>SimpleAgent</code> provides.
  </p>

  <h2 id="status">What's built</h2>
  <p>
    Sessions, tabs, views, GPU frames, input, host events (loading, titles, cursors, popups) and
    prompts for dialogs, file choosers, downloads, permissions, HTTP logins and client certificates
    are built. The <a href={`${repo}/docs/embedding/api-design.md`}>API design</a> marks what is built,
    what was checked against a real engine and what is still proposed. Not built yet: <code
      >sc_view_set_focus</code
    >, the C++ classes for the embed API, and joining an existing session from <code
      >suitecut-native-control</code
    >'s MCP server.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
